WEBVTT

00:03.880 --> 00:04.280
All right.

00:04.280 --> 00:07.160
So now let's have a look to the syntax of the set operators.

00:07.200 --> 00:07.440
Okay.

00:07.480 --> 00:09.080
So now let's see that we have the following query.

00:09.120 --> 00:11.400
We are selecting the data from the customers.

00:11.400 --> 00:15.080
So this is our first query or our first select statement.

00:15.080 --> 00:20.760
And we have another one which is very similar where we are selecting the formations from the employees.

00:20.760 --> 00:23.320
And this is our second select statement.

00:23.320 --> 00:29.520
So now what we can do we can put between those two queries a set of operators like for example the union

00:29.520 --> 00:29.960
we can use.

00:30.000 --> 00:34.920
Of course any other set operators like the union all intersects accepts and so on.

00:34.920 --> 00:37.400
So as you can see the syntax is very simple.

00:37.400 --> 00:42.160
We have two different queries and we just put between them the set operator.

00:42.160 --> 00:45.080
So this is how the syntax of the set operators looks like.

00:45.080 --> 00:45.720
All right friends.

00:45.720 --> 00:48.680
So now we're going to talk about the rules of the set operators.

00:48.680 --> 00:53.720
And we're going to start with the rule number one the SQL clauses in each individual Select statements

00:53.720 --> 00:54.320
or query.

00:54.360 --> 01:00.200
We can use almost all the SQL clauses like where join group by having.

01:00.200 --> 01:02.710
But there is only one exception with the order.

01:02.710 --> 01:08.150
By order by, you can use it only once and only at the end of the entire query.

01:08.150 --> 01:10.190
So that means we cannot use order by.

01:10.430 --> 01:13.030
In each Select statement or in each query.

01:13.070 --> 01:16.750
We can use it only once and only at the ends of the entire query.

01:16.790 --> 01:18.270
Alright, so about the syntax again.

01:18.310 --> 01:23.190
Here we have our two select statements and in between them we have the set operators.

01:23.350 --> 01:29.590
So now in each query we can go and use multiple stuff like the join where group by having.

01:29.590 --> 01:32.870
So we can make each query complex as we want.

01:32.910 --> 01:36.110
So everything is allowed but not the order by.

01:36.270 --> 01:40.750
The order by must be always placed at the end of the entire query.

01:40.750 --> 01:46.550
So if you want to sort the result by the first name, you have to use the order by exactly at the end.

01:46.670 --> 01:49.710
So we are not allowed to use order by in each query.

01:49.750 --> 01:49.950
Okay.

01:49.990 --> 01:51.350
Moving on to the rule number two.

01:51.550 --> 01:52.910
The number of columns.

01:52.910 --> 01:56.510
The number of columns on each query must be the same.

01:56.550 --> 01:56.750
Okay.

01:56.790 --> 01:59.910
So now in order to understand this rule let's have this very simple example.

01:59.950 --> 02:05.060
We're going to go and select the first name and the last name from the table.

02:05.100 --> 02:06.620
Sales customers.

02:06.620 --> 02:09.980
So this is our first query, our first select statements.

02:09.980 --> 02:12.020
And let's say that I have another one.

02:12.260 --> 02:17.300
And we want to select the first name last name but this time from another table that employees.

02:17.300 --> 02:18.940
So with that we have our two queries.

02:18.940 --> 02:22.780
And I would like now to go and combine them into one results.

02:22.780 --> 02:25.340
So we're going to go and use the set operator union.

02:25.580 --> 02:27.020
Let's go and execute it.

02:27.060 --> 02:31.020
So now as you can see in the results we will get the first name and last name from two tables, the

02:31.020 --> 02:32.660
customers and employees.

02:32.660 --> 02:37.700
And it is working because we are fulfilling the rule where it says the number of columns must be the

02:37.700 --> 02:39.260
same in both queries.

02:39.260 --> 02:41.300
So how many columns do we have in the first query?

02:41.300 --> 02:42.420
We have two, right?

02:42.660 --> 02:45.540
And as well in the second query we have two columns.

02:45.540 --> 02:47.420
So that's why everything is working.

02:47.420 --> 02:51.300
So now let's go and break the rule by adding another column to the first query.

02:51.420 --> 02:54.420
So let's say that I would like to have the customer ID as well.

02:54.460 --> 02:55.500
And the first query.

02:55.500 --> 02:59.420
And with that, as you can see in the first query we have three columns.

02:59.420 --> 03:02.000
But in the second we have only two.

03:02.040 --> 03:03.480
So let's go and execute it.

03:03.480 --> 03:03.640
Now.

03:03.640 --> 03:07.880
As you can see in the result, we will get an error where it says if you are using Union, intersect

03:07.880 --> 03:13.120
and all those operators, you must have an equal number of columns between queries.

03:13.120 --> 03:14.280
So this is the rule.

03:14.280 --> 03:17.160
You have to have the same number of columns in order to repair it.

03:17.160 --> 03:19.920
So I'm going to do I'm just going to remove the customer ID.

03:19.920 --> 03:21.400
So here again we have two columns.

03:21.400 --> 03:25.120
And the second one as well two columns and everything going to be working okay.

03:25.160 --> 03:26.760
Moving on to the rule number three.

03:26.840 --> 03:32.760
The data types of columns in each query must match must be compatible in matching.

03:32.880 --> 03:36.400
In order to check that what we're going to do, we're going to go to the object explorer to the left

03:36.400 --> 03:37.000
side.

03:37.000 --> 03:39.920
Let's go and browse the customers and the columns.

03:39.920 --> 03:43.680
And as you can see we have here the first name and last name with the same data type.

03:43.680 --> 03:44.960
We have the Varchar.

03:45.280 --> 03:50.360
And if you go to the employees you can see as well the first name last name having varchar.

03:50.520 --> 03:53.320
So the first column is a varchar from the first query.

03:53.320 --> 03:59.200
And as well for the employees and as well the last name from the customers having the same data type

03:59.200 --> 04:01.030
as the last name from employees.

04:01.030 --> 04:03.310
So the data type is matching.

04:03.510 --> 04:05.550
Now let's go and break this rule.

04:05.550 --> 04:09.510
Instead of having the first name, I would like to go and use the customer ID.

04:09.830 --> 04:15.710
So now let's check the customer ID on the left side it is an int an integer, but the first name is

04:15.710 --> 04:16.670
in varchar.

04:16.870 --> 04:19.390
So here we have a mismatch between data types.

04:19.430 --> 04:20.990
Let's go and try to execute it.

04:20.990 --> 04:26.110
So now we are getting an error where it says SQL is trying to convert the value frank to an integer.

04:26.190 --> 04:31.910
So what this means the first query is always controlling everything the names and as well the data types.

04:31.910 --> 04:33.550
So here we have an integer.

04:33.590 --> 04:38.070
And now SQL is trying as well to convert the first name values to an integer.

04:38.070 --> 04:43.150
And of course it will not work because we have here characters inside and it cannot convert characters

04:43.150 --> 04:43.950
to an integer.

04:43.950 --> 04:48.950
So we have a mismatch between data types, between the customer id and the first name.

04:48.950 --> 04:50.550
And that's why we will get an error.

04:50.750 --> 04:54.670
The second column we don't have an issue because it is Varchar and the first table.

04:54.670 --> 04:56.310
And as well for the second table.

04:56.310 --> 05:00.100
So now in order to repair it either select a first name in the first query.

05:00.100 --> 05:06.860
Or we can go over here and say employee ID and with that, if I execute it, we will not get any errors

05:06.860 --> 05:09.940
because the employee ID is as well an integer.

05:09.940 --> 05:12.100
And we have a match in the data types.

05:12.100 --> 05:16.500
So as you can see, it's not enough to have the same number of columns you have to have as well matching

05:16.500 --> 05:18.620
data types between those two queries.

05:18.660 --> 05:18.900
Okay.

05:18.900 --> 05:20.060
Let's move to the next rule.

05:20.100 --> 05:22.460
Rule number four the order of columns.

05:22.460 --> 05:26.580
The order of columns in each query must be as well the same.

05:26.620 --> 05:26.820
Okay.

05:26.860 --> 05:28.500
So let's understand what this means.

05:28.540 --> 05:32.940
Now we have here again the same example where we are selecting the id and LastName from customers.

05:32.940 --> 05:37.660
And we are combining it using union with the employee ID and LastName from the employees.

05:37.700 --> 05:42.340
And as you can see, everything is working because we have the same number of columns and we have a

05:42.340 --> 05:43.580
matching data types.

05:43.580 --> 05:44.940
So now let's go and break it.

05:44.940 --> 05:47.940
What I'm going to do I'm just going to switch between those two columns.

05:47.940 --> 05:51.660
So first I'm selecting the last name and then the customer ID.

05:51.980 --> 05:54.460
So again I have the same number of columns.

05:54.660 --> 06:00.130
And the id is integer matching the ID of the employee and the last name having the same data type.

06:00.130 --> 06:02.170
So let's go and execute it.

06:02.210 --> 06:06.810
So here again SQL is going to throw an error and says SQL is trying to convert the value Goldberg to

06:06.850 --> 06:07.570
an integer.

06:07.570 --> 06:09.130
So it's like character to integer.

06:09.130 --> 06:10.130
It will not work.

06:10.130 --> 06:12.570
So what happened here I have here the same informations.

06:12.570 --> 06:15.050
I have an ID and last name an id and last name.

06:15.050 --> 06:16.490
Well SQL doesn't work like this.

06:16.530 --> 06:21.930
SQL can go and map the first column from the first query with the first column with the second query.

06:21.930 --> 06:25.090
So it's going to go and map last name to employee ID.

06:25.130 --> 06:28.250
And since they have different data types SQL is going to throw an error.

06:28.290 --> 06:30.930
So SQL doesn't understand or don't know how to map.

06:30.930 --> 06:32.610
Let's say the id with the ID.

06:32.650 --> 06:34.170
And since they have different data types.

06:34.250 --> 06:36.130
SQL can go and throw an error.

06:36.290 --> 06:40.570
So as you can see here we have the same information between customers and employees.

06:40.810 --> 06:43.090
But they don't have the same order.

06:43.090 --> 06:47.370
So SQL cannot go and map the informations because of the names of the columns.

06:47.370 --> 06:47.970
It's going to go.

06:47.970 --> 06:50.570
And simply just mapping the columns like this.

06:50.570 --> 06:55.010
The first column from the first query with the first column from the second query.

06:55.010 --> 06:59.510
So as you can see in this rule you must have the same order of the their columns.

06:59.550 --> 07:02.950
First the id and then the last name.

07:03.190 --> 07:05.030
And with that, it's going to work again.

07:05.070 --> 07:05.390
All right.

07:05.390 --> 07:06.990
Moving on to the rule number five.

07:07.270 --> 07:13.030
The column aliases, column names that we see in the output in the result is defined and determined

07:13.030 --> 07:17.310
by the column names of the first query, the first select statements.

07:17.310 --> 07:22.310
So this means the first query is responsible of naming the columns in the output.

07:22.350 --> 07:22.630
Okay.

07:22.670 --> 07:24.470
So let's understand what this rule means.

07:24.510 --> 07:25.870
Again we have the same example.

07:25.870 --> 07:30.310
The customer ID last name from customers union employee ID last name from employees.

07:30.350 --> 07:36.110
So if you check closely the output you can see that in the output we have the customer ID and not the

07:36.110 --> 07:39.750
employee ID even though we have the IDs from the employee ID.

07:39.950 --> 07:45.390
But as you can see, the first query is controlling the naming of the output.

07:45.390 --> 07:51.310
So since the first column called the customer id, you will see it in the output as a customer ID so

07:51.310 --> 07:55.830
the naming of that like the next queries will be totally ignored.

07:55.830 --> 08:01.060
So that's why if you want to give aliases to the output, you're going to go and do it only for the

08:01.060 --> 08:01.820
first query.

08:01.820 --> 08:06.980
So for example, I go over here and say instead of having customer ID, I would like to call it as an

08:06.980 --> 08:07.460
ID.

08:07.460 --> 08:12.700
So now if I go and execute it, as you can see in the output, we will get an ID so I don't have to

08:12.740 --> 08:13.060
go.

08:13.060 --> 08:15.460
And in each query give this alias.

08:15.460 --> 08:20.940
So I don't have to go over here and say yeah you are as well the ID, because it's enough to define

08:20.940 --> 08:22.340
it from the first query.

08:22.340 --> 08:26.420
So there is no need to give the same names in the next queries.

08:26.460 --> 08:29.620
Let's take another example where we would like to have an alias for the last name.

08:29.620 --> 08:33.420
So I would like to have it like this last underscore name.

08:33.620 --> 08:35.900
And let's go and do it in the second query.

08:36.100 --> 08:38.660
So last underscore name.

08:38.980 --> 08:40.700
Let's go and execute it.

08:40.700 --> 08:43.420
So now as you can see in the output we still have last name.

08:43.420 --> 08:47.500
And there is no underscore because this is totally ignored from SQL.

08:47.540 --> 08:49.380
This is not the first query.

08:49.540 --> 08:53.100
The first query says you are last name without underscore.

08:53.100 --> 08:55.380
So again if you want to do that we go over here.

08:55.380 --> 08:58.530
Let me just get it and put it in the first query.

08:58.570 --> 08:59.930
Let's go and execute it.

08:59.930 --> 09:04.850
So my friends, the first query is very important in order to give the names for the output.

09:04.850 --> 09:09.410
So if you want to do aliases and to rename stuff, do it only on the first query.

09:09.450 --> 09:12.290
And as well the first query controls the data types.

09:12.330 --> 09:12.610
All right.

09:12.650 --> 09:15.850
Now to the last rule matching the correct informations.

09:15.850 --> 09:21.210
If in your query you fulfill all other rules and you don't have an error in the SQL, that doesn't mean

09:21.210 --> 09:24.090
that your result is accurate and correct.

09:24.090 --> 09:29.370
You are the only one that is responsible of mapping the information between queries correctly.

09:29.370 --> 09:35.330
Because SQL doesn't understand the content and the informations of your tables of your queries, and

09:35.330 --> 09:40.650
if you don't match the informations correctly between the queries, you will get inaccurate and wrong

09:40.650 --> 09:41.890
results in the output.

09:41.930 --> 09:43.250
Okay, so now back to our example.

09:43.290 --> 09:49.930
Let's say I would like to get the first name and as well the last name from the customers and the same

09:49.930 --> 09:51.930
informations from the employees.

09:51.970 --> 09:53.450
Let's go and execute it.

09:53.450 --> 09:53.610
Now.

09:53.610 --> 09:58.640
As you can see it's very nice where we are getting the first name, last name from both tables in one

09:58.640 --> 10:01.480
result and we are fulfilling all the requirements in SQL.

10:01.520 --> 10:03.360
Same numbers, same data types and so on.

10:03.400 --> 10:05.520
Now let's go and make incorrect results.

10:05.520 --> 10:10.800
So what I'm going to do I'm just going to swap the first name and last name in the second query.

10:11.080 --> 10:13.760
So first last name and then the first name.

10:14.120 --> 10:15.760
So let's go and execute it.

10:15.760 --> 10:20.240
So now as you can see we will get results because we are fulfilling all other rules because we have

10:20.240 --> 10:21.640
the same number of columns.

10:21.640 --> 10:24.120
And as well we have matching data types.

10:24.120 --> 10:28.560
So the first one is character the first name and the last name is as well character.

10:28.560 --> 10:31.880
So SQL will just present the result as you define it.

10:31.880 --> 10:37.480
But the result is completely wrong because now we have if you check the first column here the first

10:37.520 --> 10:38.000
name.

10:38.000 --> 10:42.760
So here we can see last names inside the first names for example Brown and Baker.

10:43.000 --> 10:47.760
Those are last names, but we can see them inside the first name and the same thing in the last name.

10:47.880 --> 10:51.480
Now we can see first names inside it Mary, Carol.

10:51.520 --> 10:53.000
They are all first names.

10:53.000 --> 10:55.640
So as you can see the result has really bad data quality.

10:55.640 --> 10:58.820
We are now mixing stuff and it doesn't make any sense.

10:58.820 --> 11:04.860
But SQL will not know that because SQL doesn't know the information, the content of your data.

11:05.340 --> 11:06.900
It's just mapping the data types.

11:06.900 --> 11:09.020
So firstName is a varchar.

11:09.060 --> 11:09.980
The last name as well.

11:10.020 --> 11:10.500
Varchar.

11:10.540 --> 11:12.980
Everything is fine and you will get the results.

11:12.980 --> 11:19.100
So my friends, you are responsible of having the same informations mapped between the two queries.

11:19.140 --> 11:23.060
And not having an error from SQL doesn't mean that we have now correct results.

11:23.060 --> 11:27.940
So pay attention to the informations that you are mapping between the two queries.

11:27.980 --> 11:28.340
All right.

11:28.340 --> 11:30.900
So those are the rules of the set operators.

11:30.900 --> 11:36.260
So the first one is that the order by can only be used once at the end of the entire query.

11:36.260 --> 11:43.220
And all queries must have the same number of columns, the matching data types, the same order of columns

11:43.220 --> 11:49.060
and the first query always control the names and the aliases of the result set, and as well the data

11:49.100 --> 11:49.580
type.

11:49.580 --> 11:55.380
And the last rule of that, make sure that you are mapping the correct informations to each others between

11:55.380 --> 11:56.060
queries.

11:56.060 --> 11:58.380
So those are the rules of the set operators.
